Owned hardware and rented capacity can both be called a GPU, but that label hides almost everything that matters for production: which exact card, how it is connected, how much memory is really usable, whether another tenant can see your data, whether the machine will still be there tomorrow, and what it costs when it fails.

Some things can honestly be abstracted away. The rest of the company should never have to think about BMC passwords, PXE boot order, driver changelogs, or which remote-hands technician swapped a drive. Those are yours to own.

Some things have to stay visible, because hiding them creates a false promise. GPU model and memory, interconnect, isolation level, data locality, failure domain, cost, and availability cannot be hidden. If you hide them, a user will write a workload that assumes NVLink, gets PCIe, assumes isolation, gets shared memory, or assumes permanent capacity, gets preempted, and then blames the platform.

And some things should be visible only as a decision, not as a detail. A user should be able to say: I need 8 GPUs, this isolation level, this data locality, this max cost, this max wait. They should not have to say: I need a node with this BIOS version and this driver build in this rack.

That three-way split is the job. Abstract the mechanics, surface the constraints, hide the toil.

The hardware end is about trust without physical access.
When capacity arrives as hardware, you get everything that implies: firmware versions that interact in undocumented ways, drivers that only work with certain kernels and CUDA versions, hosts that need bring-up, and physical work done by people you will never meet in buildings you will rarely visit.

You cannot debug that by walking over to the machine. You debug it by discipline: out-of-band console and BMC logs before you reboot away the evidence, kernel logs over the network for cases where userspace is dead, a known-good node to compare against, and a health gate before a node returns to service. That is the same discipline as your dead filer case: the symptom looked like a scheduler problem, the layer was storage, and the proof was in tracing it across layers instead of restarting the loudest service.

It also means remote hands should never be asked to debug. They should be asked to do one precise physical action, based on diagnostics you already gathered: replace this FRU, reseat this cable, check this power feed. If your ticket says "machine is broken, please investigate," you have already lost.

Acceptance is part of this too. A machine is not production capacity because a purchase order was paid. It is production capacity after it boots clean, passes a GPU diagnostic, matches the firmware and driver matrix, passes a test job, and reports correctly to monitoring. Everything before that is a long tail of wrong SKUs, BIOS settings, cabling, and cooling.

The provider end is about control without ownership.
With a neocloud or another provider, you control the software and very little else, on terms set by someone whose interests are not yours. Their interests are utilization, standardization, and margin. Yours are performance, isolation, availability, and cost for a specific workload.

That means you cannot trust the advertised spec. You have to measure it: what GPU is actually attached, what interconnect is actually present, what bandwidth you actually get, whether the driver is actually the version you need, and whether capacity in that region is actually available when you need it, not just when you tested it.

Capacity that is not permanent changes design. Preemptible or short-lived capacity is fine for fault-tolerant batch with checkpointing, and terrible for a long training run with no checkpoint. Provider APIs also fail in provider-specific ways: rate limits, quota errors, region outages, maintenance windows. Those are not node failures, and they should not trigger node quarantine logic. They should trigger retry, fallback to another provider or to owned capacity, and honest accounting of what the job actually cost.

This is also where your thinner end is, and it is worth naming plainly. You have not run a neocloud in production, you have not run production Terraform, and your virtualization depth is VMware and OpenStack, not production KVM. What you do have is the layer underneath the provider API: Linux, drivers, firmware, BMC, networking, and GPU provisioning. That is actually the right foundation for judging provider claims, because you know what the API is hiding. The interview version of that is not "I have done neoclouds." It is "I have operated the thing neoclouds abstract, so I know what to measure before I trust it."

Choosing bare metal, VM, or container is a placement decision, not a religion.
The right answer changes because the workload changes. Long, topology-sensitive training wants bare metal. Untrusted multi-tenant work wants a VM or a whole node, not a shared container. Trusted internal batch can run containers on bare metal with device controls. Short inference can be containers.

The mistake is to pick one and defend it. The job is to make placement explicit and revisitable: this workload got bare metal because of this interconnect and this isolation requirement, recorded somewhere, so when the workload or the price changes, the decision can change without a redesign.

And the last line matters most: this is not architecture-only.
A diagram that has never been on call is not infrastructure. The loop is design, build, debug, operate, and then redesign based on what broke. Your node broker is a good example of that loop: drain-first ordering, never open on both sides, never kill jobs, health gates before handover. Those rules did not come from a whiteboard. They came from what goes wrong when two schedulers share nodes and nobody owns the transition.

If you want to test this the way a CTO would, the uncomfortable question is the provider one: a provider offers you the same GPU SKU at a lower price, in a region you have never used. What do you measure first, before you move a real workload there?
